\description{
Simulates choice reaction times from a two-accumulator Racing Diffusion Model (RDM).
This is a specialized version where exactly two accumulators race towards a
common threshold. The model assumes variability in the starting point of the
diffusion process, drawn from a uniform distribution. This version is optimized
for performance using vectorized operations, and allows drift rates to be zero
(a zero-drift accumulator is slow but still finishes; see Details).
}
\details{
The RDM implemented here follows the formulation where the two accumulators
have drift rates \code{vzero} and \code{vone}. The diffusion process starts at a point \code{z} drawn
from \code{Uniform(0, bias)}. The process terminates when either accumulator reaches
a threshold \code{b}. The parameter \code{bs} is defined as \code{bs = b - bias}, representing
the distance from the maximum starting point \code{bias} to the threshold \code{b}.
Therefore, the effective distance to threshold for a given trial is
\code{bs = b - z = bs + bias - z}.

The finishing time for a single accumulator, given its drift rate \code{v}, \code{bs}, \code{bias}, and \code{ndt},
is simulated by:
\enumerate{
\item Sampling a starting point \code{z ~ Uniform(0, bias)}.
\item Calculating the distance \code{bs = bs + bias - z}.
\item If \code{v > 0}, simulating the time to reach \code{bs} from an Inverse Gaussian distribution
with mean \code{bs / v} and shape \code{bs^2}. This simulation uses an
internal implementation based on Michael et al. (1976).
\item If \code{v = 0}, drawing the driftless first passage time, which is Levy
distributed: \code{bs^2 / Z^2} with \code{Z ~ Normal(0, 1)}. A zero-drift
accumulator is slow, but it still finishes with probability one, so it can
win the race.
\item Adding the non-decision time \code{ndt} to the finishing times.
}

The function simulates this process for both accumulators using vectorized operations.
The accumulator that finishes first determines the response (0 for \code{vzero}, 1 for \code{vone})
and the reaction time (RT) for that trial.

This implementation is based on the description and parameters used in:
Tillman, G., Van Zandt, T., & Logan, G. D. (2020). Sequential sampling models
without random between-trial variability: The racing diffusion model of
speeded decision making. \emph{Psychonomic Bulletin & Review}, \emph{27}, 911-936.
\doi{10.3758/s13423-020-01738-8} (specifically matching the \code{WaldA} component
used within their RDM simulation).

\code{prdm()} describes the RT of the race as a whole (whichever accumulator
wins), since \code{P(min(T0, T1) > q) = S0(q) * S1(q)}. There is no comparably
simple closed form for the per-response defective CDF, so \code{prdm()} takes no
\code{response} argument.

The \code{brms} family names the drift of the first accumulator \code{mu} (as \code{brms}
requires) and that of the second \code{driftone}, and calls the start-point range
\code{sigmabias} to match \code{\link[=lba]{lba()}}, where it denotes the same quantity. Note that
this is \emph{not} the same thing as \code{bias} in \code{\link[=ddm]{ddm()}}, which is a relative
starting point in \verb{[0, 1]}. Both drifts use a softplus link with a lower
bound of zero, following \code{\link[=rt_invgaussian]{rt_invgaussian()}}: a Wald drift must be
non-negative for the accumulator to be a proper first passage time.

Note that \code{sigmabias} and \code{bs} are only weakly identified from each other,
because they enter the threshold as the sum \code{b = bs + sigmabias} and trade
off almost freely: on simulated data with 4000 trials the profile
log-likelihood varies by only about 3 units as \code{sigmabias} ranges from 0 to
half the threshold, while \code{bs} slides to compensate. With flat priors the
sampler tends to wander down the \code{sigmabias -> 0} ridge (the plain Wald race)
and produce divergent transitions. A weakly informative prior on
\code{sigmabias} fixes this, for example
\code{brms::prior(normal(-1, 1), class = "Intercept", dpar = "sigmabias")}, which
under the softplus link is centred near \code{0.31}. The sum \code{bs + sigmabias} is
well identified either way, so it is the more trustworthy quantity to
interpret and to compare across conditions. The same caveat applies to
\code{\link[=lba]{lba()}}, which shares this parameterisation.
}
